      "snapshot": "Short Range Forecast Discussion\nNWS Weather Prediction Center College Park MD\n300 AM EST Sun Nov 8 2026\n\nValid 12Z Sun Nov 08 2026 - 12Z Tue Nov 10 2026\n\n...Heavy rainfall threat peaks across southern Texas today into\nMonday morning...\n\n...Periods of showers and gusty winds for the Pacific Northwest\nover the next few days...\n\n...Seasonably mild to warm temperatures for most of the country but\nmorning frost/freeze across the Ohio Valley to Mid-South...\n\nA relatively compact upper-level low interacting with lower-level \nmoisture from the Gulf is triggering numerous showers and\nthunderstorms early this morning from the Rio Grande Valley to\ncentral Texas. The greatest threat of heavy rainfall associated\nwith thunderstorm clusters is expected to focus across southern\nTexas for today through tonight before gradually lessening during\nthe day on Monday. WPC continues to highlight a Slight Risk of\nExcessive Rainfall (level 2/4) for southern Texas where rainfall\ntotals of several inches will raise the threat of scattered flash\nflooding. Additional but less heavy showers are expected to linger\ninto Tuesday morning.\n\nAcross New England, scattered showers on the back side of a\ndeveloping low pressure system will swing across the region today.\nThe higher elevation of interior northern New England could see wet\nsnow mixing in with the rain. Maine will be the last to clear out\nfrom the chilly rain as the system exits into the Canadian\nMaritimes late today. However, another clipper system across\nsouthern Canada will quickly bring the next round of showers across\nthe Great Lakes today, reaching into upstate New York and interior\nNew England by this evening. Some more showers are expected to pop\nup on Monday across upstate New York and interior New England\nbefore the upper low moves away Monday night.\n\nMeanwhile, a couple of strong Pacific cyclones will bring rounds\nof moderate rain and some higher elevation mountain snow into the\nPacific Northwest. The first system has already made landfall early\nthis morning. The associated line of showers will taper off to\nlight rain later today, followed by mostly dry weather on Monday,\nbefore the next round of unsettled weather arrives later on Monday\ninto Monday night. Some very gusty winds will also be possible,\nparticularly along the coast and higher elevations in the Pacific\nNorthwest. In addition, very gusty downslope winds are forecast\nfor northern Montana for today.\n\nOtherwise, most of the country will see dry conditions with\nseasonably mild to warm temperatures through the remainder of the\nweekend and into early next week under the influence of a high\npressure system. High temperatures will generally be 5-15 degrees\nabove average, with 50s and 60s along the northern tier and 60s to\n70s along the southern tier. Some of the greatest anomalies will be\nin place across the northern High Plains southeast into the\nMissouri Valley as highs reach well into the 60s to low 70s, and\nacross southern California as highs climb well into the 80s. In\ncontrast, chilly mornings with lows around freezing are expected\nacross the Mid- South to Ohio/Tennessee Valleys where Frost/Freeze-\nrelated advisories are in place. One location that will run\nrelatively cooler and below average will be across southern/western\nTexas and into portions of the Southwest with the passage of the\nupper-wave due to widespread clouds and precipitation. Highs will\nbe mostly in the lower to mid-60s with some locations only rising\ninto the 50s.\n\n\nKong/Putnam\n\n\nGraphics available at\n<a href=\"https://www.wpc.ncep.noaa.gov/basicwx/basicwx_ndfd.php\"><u>https://www.wpc.ncep.noaa.gov/basicwx/basicwx_ndfd.php</u></a>"
    },
