      "snapshot": "Extended Forecast Discussion\nNWS Weather Prediction Center College Park MD\n251 AM EST Sun Nov 8 2026\n\nValid 12Z Wed Nov 11 2026 - 12Z Sun Nov 15 2026\n\n\n...Overview...\n\nThe upper level pattern across the nation will trend more amplified\ngoing into the second half of the week and into next weekend. A\nbuilding upper level ridge will be in place from the southern\nPlains to central Canada, with above normal temperatures associated\nwith it. Multiple shortwaves and cold fronts brushing past the\nPacific Northwest coastline will result in multiple days of\nunsettled conditions, including some potential atmospheric river\nevents. The eastern U.S. will also have a low pressure system by\nthe end of the week as the upper trough intensifies across the East\nCoast states, bringing a cold rain and potential inland snow for\nthe interior Northeast.\n\n\n...Guidance/Predictability Assessment...\n\nThe 00Z model guidance suite is in reasonably good agreement on the\nsynoptic scale pattern for the beginning of the forecast period\nWednesday. There is improving agreement on the East Coast storm\nsystem for Friday, although the CMC remains less intense than the\nstronger GFS and ECMWF solutions, but still excellent agreement on\nthe upper ridge axis across the Plains and Rockies. More mesoscale\ndifferences exist by next weekend, although the general flow\npattern remains well depicted in the guidance. The ensemble means\nwere gradually increased to about 40-50% of the model blend by next\nSunday.\n\n\n...Weather/Hazards Highlights...\n\nMultiple rounds of rainfall are becoming more likely for western\nWashington and Oregon as a large scale trough develops over the\nnortheast Pacific, with multiple shortwaves and frontal boundaries\ntracking towards British Columbia and bringing periods of rainfall\nthrough Friday. A more organized storm system is likely to affect\nmore of the West Coast going into Saturday and into Sunday, with\nheavier rainfall and high mountain snow reaching farther south into\nnorthern California. A weaker southern stream trough may bring\nlighter rainfall across portions of the Southwest and into southern\nTexas Friday into Saturday.\n\nAn amplifying upper trough over the Great Lakes and Midwest will\nlead to the development of a surface low across the Mid-Atlantic\nregion by Thursday, and this should develop further as it exits the\nEast Coast by Friday with the potential for a nor'easter to develop\nas the low tracks offshore. There is still uncertainty in the track\nand expected impacts, but precipitation prospects have increased\nover the past 24 hours with rain east of the Appalachians, and\nearly season snow possible for the higher terrain across the\ninterior Northeast and into Pennsylvania. Some lake effect rain and\nsnow showers are also likely in the wake of this storm system.\n\nTemperatures will initially be mild across most of the country for\nthe middle of the week, but this changes going into Thursday and\neven more so by Friday for areas east of the Mississippi River as\nthe upper trough takes hold and leads to below average temperatures\nfrom the Southeast U.S. to the Mid-Atlantic region. Highs should be\n5-15 degrees below normal before a gradual moderation trend ensues\nnext weekend. The opposite will hold true across the central and\nnorthern Plains with above average temperatures expected most days\nwith the upper level ridge in place. Highs could be 10-20 degrees\nabove mid-November averages.\n\nHamrick\n\n\nAdditional 3-7 Day Hazard information can be found on the WPC\nmedium range hazards outlook chart at:\n<a href=\"https://www.wpc.ncep.noaa.gov/threats/threats.php\"><u>https://www.wpc.ncep.noaa.gov/threats/threats.php</u></a>\n\nWPC medium range 500mb heights, surface systems, weather grids,\nquantitative precipitation forecast, excessive rainfall outlook,\nwinter weather outlook probabilities, heat indices and Key Messages\nare at:\n\n<a href=\"https://www.wpc.ncep.noaa.gov/medr/5dayfcst500_wbg.gif\"><u>https://www.wpc.ncep.noaa.gov/medr/5dayfcst500_wbg.gif</u></a>\n<a href=\"https://www.wpc.ncep.noaa.gov/medr/5dayfcst_wbg_conus.gif\"><u>https://www.wpc.ncep.noaa.gov/medr/5dayfcst_wbg_conus.gif</u></a>\n<a href=\"https://www.wpc.ncep.noaa.gov/5km_grids/5km_gridsbody.html\"><u>https://www.wpc.ncep.noaa.gov/5km_grids/5km_gridsbody.html</u></a>\n<a href=\"https://www.wpc.ncep.noaa.gov/qpf/day4-7.shtml\"><u>https://www.wpc.ncep.noaa.gov/qpf/day4-7.shtml</u></a>\n<a href=\"https://www.wpc.ncep.noaa.gov/#page=ero\"><u>https://www.wpc.ncep.noaa.gov/#page=ero</u></a>\n<a href=\"https://www.wpc.ncep.noaa.gov/wwd/pwpf_d47/pwpf_medr.php?day=4\"><u>https://www.wpc.ncep.noaa.gov/wwd/pwpf_d47/pwpf_medr.php?day=4</u></a>\n<a href=\"https://www.wpc.ncep.noaa.gov/heat_index.shtml\"><u>https://www.wpc.ncep.noaa.gov/heat_index.shtml</u></a>\n<a href=\"https://www.wpc.ncep.noaa.gov/#page=ovw\"><u>https://www.wpc.ncep.noaa.gov/#page=ovw</u></a>"
